WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:05.529
Things normal people are afraid of: zombies,
the dark, spiders, and whether or not the

00:00:05.529 --> 00:00:07.259
waiter spit in our food.

00:00:07.259 --> 00:00:08.430
"Take it back!

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:12.780
If I wanted something your thumb touched I'd
eat the inside of your ear!"

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:14.459
Things celebrities are afraid of?

00:00:14.459 --> 00:00:16.750
Sketchy acting jobs.

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:23.000
Sometimes actors pass up blockbusters because
of the overall creepiness of the movie, a

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.560
fear of awful writing, or just because they're
afraid of disappointing their fans.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:33.770
Here are a few famous actors who have turned
down incredible roles for totally legitimate

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:37.390
reasons — and some who lived to totally
regret it.

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:39.739
Michelle Pfeiffer: Silence of the Lambs

00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:44.850
Even a legend like Michelle Pfeiffer isn't
afraid to admit she was too afraid to take

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:47.649
on the subject matter of Silence of the Lambs.

00:00:47.649 --> 00:00:53.180
As Empire revealed in its look at the making
of the movie, she was turned off by overwhelming

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:58.430
darkness and violence in the script — a
letdown for director Jonathan Demme, who'd

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:03.989
worked with her previously and made Pfeiffer
his first choice for the role of Clarice Starling.

00:01:03.989 --> 00:01:07.980
He went through a number of other actresses
before hiring Jodie Foster.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:12.550
Honestly, we wouldn't want to be face-to-face
with Hannibal Lecter even if we were just

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:16.540
on a movie set, so it's easy to sympathize
with Pfeiffer.

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:21.730
Meanwhile, another big star passed on a role
in Silence of the Lambs: Jeremy Irons.

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:26.630
Apparently, playing a cannibal serial killer
is just a little worse than the creepy dude

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:28.230
he played in Lolita.

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:30.590
Josh Hartnett: Superman Returns

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:36.060
At the ripe age of 22, Josh Hartnett was afraid
of being pigeonholed when he was asked to

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:40.220
wear the red cape in the 2006 film Superman
Returns.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:45.030
So he turned down the role, partially because
he valued his free time, but also for fear

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:46.940
of damaging his career.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:52.040
Also in Hartnett's "thanks, but no thanks"
pile: opportunities to play both Batman and

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:53.040
Spider-Man.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:57.770
As he later admitted to Playboy, choosing
not to put on the tights is what ultimately

00:01:57.770 --> 00:01:59.890
hurt his career after all, saying,

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:02.600
"I've definitely said no to some of the wrong
people.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:07.960
I said no because I was tired and wanted to
spend more time with my friends and family.

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:09.970
That's frowned upon in this industry.

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:11.700
People don't like being told no."

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:14.390
Jude Law: Superman Returns

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:17.700
English actor Jude Law grappled with the same
offer as Hartnett.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:22.610
Director Bryan Singer considered going with
the British star, but Law wasn't sure pinning

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:24.250
on the cape was a good idea.

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:28.840
He was worried about his nationality, thinking
an Englishman shouldn't play the guy who stands

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:33.030
for the American way — and he also wasn't
crazy about putting on the tights.

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:38.600
Hoping to sway the actor, Singer had the costume
sent over, and Law tried it on for size.

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:43.000
"I take the suit into the bathroom and I'm
putting it on…and I look around… and I

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.380
look in the mirror, and suddenly I'm Superman."

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:50.160
Law even started hearing the original film's
John Williams theme — but the fantasy didn't

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:51.160
last long.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:55.810
"And then I had this picture of that..of me
in that you know on posters all around the

00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:58.680
world and I thought… 'no way' and I unzipped
it."

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:03.160
Suddenly, there was "no way" he could play
the part, although he did have the satisfaction

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:05.460
of pretending to be Superman for a few moments.

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:08.140
And as Law put it, "That was enough."

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:10.150
Bette Midler: Sister Act

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:15.430
It's hard to picture anyone but Whoopi Goldberg
as Sister Mary Clarence in Sister Act, but

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:17.860
the role was first offered to Bette Midler.

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:22.400
As she later told Metro, she turned down the
opportunity because she was afraid of how

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:25.720
her fans would react to her portrayal of a
nun, saying,

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:28.510
"My fans don't want to see me in a wimple."

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:33.440
Who wants to break the news to Midler that
the character wasn't actually a nun?

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:37.010
That's kinda what the "Act" part in the title
meant.

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:39.150
Jennifer Hudson: Precious

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:43.830
Dreamgirls star Jennifer Hudson was offered
the title role in Precious, but turned it

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:49.030
down — a decision she initially attributed
to a reluctance to gain the weight necessary

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:50.190
for the character.

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:55.120
She later insisted the decision actually had
more to do with what was on the page than

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.720
what would've shown up on the scale, telling
The Huffington Post,

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:02.880
"I'm a firm believer that what is meant for
you is meant for you.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:05.870
I did not turn the role down because of the
weight situation.

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:10.930
I just felt the character was doing things,
at least in my script that I got, that were

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:15.800
places I did not want to go and not where
I needed to go."

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:18.160
Mark Wahlberg: Brokeback Mountain

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:23.180
Originally offered the role of Jack Twist
in Brokeback Mountain, Mark Wahlberg declined

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:28.450
the part after feeling, quote, "creeped out"
by the script, and letting his fear of the

00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:31.870
movie's homosexual themes outweigh the potential
of the role.

00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:36.160
The National Enquirer doubled down on the
rumors and stated that Wahlberg was simply

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:38.710
told 'no' by his priest.

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:40.340
It's acting, Mark!

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:41.340
"What?

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:42.340
No!"

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:46.740
Jake Gyllenhaal and Heath Ledger signed on,
and Gyllenhaal's Oscar nomination for the

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:50.190
film must have been fun for Wahlberg to read
about.

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:52.270
Brad Pitt: 12 Years a Slave

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:57.020
As one of the most acclaimed dramas to come
out of the major-studio system in recent years,

00:04:57.020 --> 00:05:02.960
12 Years a Slave almost starred Brad Pitt
in the villainous role of Edwin Epps.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:07.120
Pitt told The Mirror that he refused the role
because he was afraid of what his children

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:10.360
would think if they saw him as a slavemaster
in the film.

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:15.070
He opted for a cameo role — because, as
one of the producers on the movie, you get

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:16.530
to do things like that.

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:18.410
John Lithgow: The Fly

00:05:18.410 --> 00:05:22.460
David Cronenberg's The Fly is pretty much
a perfect horror movie.

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:27.530
It's disgustingly awesome, and Jeff Goldblum
and Geena Davis totally kill it as the two

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:28.530
leads.

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:32.330
It's hard to imagine anyone else playing their
parts, but shockingly, Goldblum wasn't the

00:05:32.330 --> 00:05:36.340
only actor in consideration for this grotesque
manimal.

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:41.460
As it turns out, John Lithgow was also up
for a trip through the teleportation pods.

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:43.949
Lithgow is no stranger to playing monsters.

00:05:43.949 --> 00:05:48.760
But despite his penchant for playing scary
characters, Lithgow didn't have the stomach

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:51.300
to morph into a man-sized fly.

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:55.560
In an interview with In Magazine, the actor
admitted his agent wanted him to play the

00:05:55.560 --> 00:06:01.890
doomed scientist Seth Brundle, but Lithgow
thought The Fly was an "icky story," and he

00:06:01.890 --> 00:06:04.060
"didn't want to play something so grotesque."

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:09.240
" Oh.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:11.000
That's disgusting."

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:15.560
And if the guy who played the Trinity Killer
thought a movie was too gross, then you should

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:17.780
probably be afraid of watching that film.

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:19.720
Be very afraid.

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:21.580
Will Ferrell: Reagan

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:26.300
Even before the film Reagan began filming,
the project was dead in the water due to public

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:27.300
outrage.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:33.310
In this alternate history take on Ronald Reagan's
last four years in office, the 40th commander-in-chief

00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:38.870
is suffering from Alzheimer's during his second
term, and one intern's given the job of tricking

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:43.190
Reagan into thinking he's still an actor,
and is actually playing the president in a

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:44.190
film.

00:06:44.190 --> 00:06:48.380
When word leaked out that Will Ferrell, no
stranger to playing presidents, was considering

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:53.810
playing the dementia-stricken Reagan, the
internet went absolutely insane.

00:06:53.810 --> 00:06:58.940
Most notably, the movie also drew criticism
from the Reagan family, who claimed the film

00:06:58.940 --> 00:07:01.790
was offensive to people suffering from Alzheimer's.

00:07:01.790 --> 00:07:06.310
Things got so intense that director Adam McKay
claimed people were calling the production

00:07:06.310 --> 00:07:08.330
offices and leaving threatening voicemails.

00:07:08.330 --> 00:07:13.300
Soon, anger at the project reached such a
fever pitch that Ferrell essentially killed

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:17.560
the film by announcing he was no longer interested
in the part.

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:18.950
Probably for the best.

00:07:18.950 --> 00:07:21.400
Toshiro Mifune: Star Wars

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:26.199
By now, everybody knows the original Star
Wars was a brilliant mash-up of George Lucas's

00:07:26.199 --> 00:07:27.470
favorite stuff.

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:33.020
Throw in some Flash Gordon, mix in a bit of
World War II footage, add a dash of The Searchers,

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:34.800
and presto, you've got A New Hope.

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:39.870
But perhaps the biggest influences on the
Star Wars saga were the films of Akira Kurosawa.

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:46.139
Of course, you can't have a Kurosawa film
without Toshiro Mifune, a superstar who appeared

00:07:46.139 --> 00:07:48.130
in 16 Kurosawa films.

00:07:48.130 --> 00:07:53.949
He made such an impression on Lucas that the
director wanted him to play Obi-Wan Kenobi.

00:07:53.949 --> 00:07:59.160
There's no denying Mifune would've made an
excellent Jedi knight, but according to his

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:04.000
daughter, Mifune was worried that the film,
quote, "...would cheapen the image of the

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:05.000
samurai.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:09.210
At the time, sci-fi movies still looked quite
cheap as the effects were not advanced, and

00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:11.190
he had a lot of samurai pride."

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:16.750
Mifune waved his hand and told Lucas to move
along, to both the roles of Kenobi and Darth

00:08:16.750 --> 00:08:18.370
Vader, according to his daughter.

00:08:18.370 --> 00:08:23.280
If that's true, his samurai pride cost him
a pretty hefty paycheck.

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:25.250
Chris Evans: Captain America

00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:30.030
It's pretty hard to imagine anyone other than
Chris Evans playing Captain America.

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:33.949
Evans seems to embody Cap's innate goodness,
nobility, and heroism.

00:08:33.949 --> 00:08:39.110
It's really a case of having just the right
actor in just the right role, and that makes

00:08:39.110 --> 00:08:43.500
it all the more surprising when you realize
Evans initially turned down the Marvel super

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:44.500
serum.

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:47.980
On Jimmy Kimmel Live!, Evans admitted that
he was afraid of the whole thing.

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:53.161
"I was scared because you know at first it
was some nine-picture contract, and there

00:08:53.161 --> 00:08:56.540
are parts of me that have a little bit of
social anxiety with this industry."

00:08:56.540 --> 00:08:59.050
He turned Marvel down…twice.

00:08:59.050 --> 00:09:03.449
As he explained to The Scotsman, he just enjoyed
doing smaller films and staying under the

00:09:03.449 --> 00:09:04.449
radar.

00:09:04.449 --> 00:09:09.990
But eventually, the star-spangled actor realized
he was "saying no out of fear," and he finally

00:09:09.990 --> 00:09:14.720
picked up the vibranium shield after realizing
he was turning it down for the wrong reasons.

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:17.149
"...whatever you're scared of, push yourself
into it."

00:09:17.149 --> 00:09:20.410
Which sounds just like something Captain America
might say.

00:09:20.410 --> 00:09:22.399
Chris Pratt: Guardians of the Galaxy

00:09:22.399 --> 00:09:27.860
We know Chris Pratt eventually took the starring
role of Star-Lord in Guardians of the Galaxy,

00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:32.380
but before he helped save the universe, he
originally passed on the part.

00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:36.880
As he told Entertainment Weekly, he thought
he was too fat to play a superhero, and he

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:41.629
was ultimately afraid of the same feelings
of rejection he dealt with when he was passed

00:09:41.629 --> 00:09:43.959
over for Star Trek and Avatar.

00:09:43.959 --> 00:09:49.769
A huge fan of the action movie genre, Pratt
later faced his fears and won the hearts of

00:09:49.769 --> 00:09:52.029
Guardians fans everywhere.

00:09:52.029 --> 00:09:55.759
That right there is a real-life superhero
story.

00:09:55.759 --> 00:09:58.110
Denzel Washington: Se7en

00:09:58.110 --> 00:10:03.019
Speaking with GQ, Denzel Washington admitted
he regretted turning down the chance to star

00:10:03.019 --> 00:10:08.160
in Se7en, the David Fincher classic about
a serial killer who uses the seven deadly

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:10.560
sins as a template for his murders.

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:15.689
As he explained, he found the script "too
dark and evil" — although now that we've

00:10:15.689 --> 00:10:19.819
all had a couple of decades to love Brad Pitt
and Morgan Freeman in the movie, he feels

00:10:19.819 --> 00:10:21.459
a little better about the film.

00:10:21.459 --> 00:10:25.709
If you ever see Washington, don't forget to
ask him "what's in the box."

00:10:25.709 --> 00:10:28.339
"What's in the boooox?"What's in the boooox?"

00:10:28.339 --> 00:10:30.360
"Because I envy your normal life, David."

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:32.199
"Put the gun down put the gun down, David"

00:10:32.199 --> 00:10:33.339
"Seems that envy is my sin."

00:10:33.339 --> 00:10:34.889
Ah what's in the booooox?"

00:10:34.889 --> 00:10:36.279
"It's a box of balloons."

00:10:36.279 --> 00:10:37.279
"It's balloons!"

00:10:37.279 --> 00:10:38.290
"It's just balloons."

00:10:38.290 --> 00:10:39.369
"It's balloons."

00:10:39.369 --> 00:10:41.250
"It's a box of balloons."

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:42.959
"There must be a hundred balloons in here!"

00:10:42.959 --> 00:10:43.959
Thanks for watching!

00:10:43.959 --> 00:10:46.630
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:10:46.630 --> 00:10:49.410
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

